<!--
The scripting page, booting `beet-full.wasm`: the ceiling of the browser feature
range, ie every feature a browser binary can boot with, from the example surface and
the perceive-act head through the agent thread runtime to the embedded JavaScript
engine. This page runs a program the floor cannot: `examples/action/scripting.bsx`,
whose leaves are JavaScript, needs both the engine and the `<NumberScript>` /
`<TextScript>` templates that ride it. Build it with `just build-wasm-full`; until
then the page loads and the module fetch 404s.

Structurally identical to `/` (a full `<HtmlDocument>`, `<RenderConsole>` before
`<Wasm>` so the console wrap installs first), differing only in which artifact and
which program it names.
-->
<HtmlDocument>
	<Fragment slot="head">
		<Stylesheet/>
		<ColorSchemeScript/>
		<LiveReloadScript/>
	</Fragment>
	<h1>Scripting in the browser</h1>
	<p>
		A behavior tree whose leaves are JavaScript, running in your browser. The
		engine is compiled into the wasm binary, so a script has no ambient authority:
		no fetch, no host globals, no page. Its console output renders below.
	</p>
	<p>
		This binary is the kitchen sink, so it also carries everything else that
		compiles for the browser. The <a href="/">home page</a> boots the other end of
		the range, <code>beet-min.wasm</code>, which carries the runtime and nothing
		more.
	</p>
	<RenderConsole/>
	<MainBsx src="/examples/action/scripting.bsx"/>
	<Wasm src="/assets/wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
</HtmlDocument>
